From tru64-unix-managers  Wed May 30 15:06:02 2001
From: Eric.Chacron () alcatel ! fr
Date: Wed, 30 May 2001 15:06:02 +0000
To: tru64-unix-managers
Subject: disk drive accesses performances
X-MARC-Message: https://marc.info/?l=tru64-unix-managers&m=102653647622187



Hi every-body,


On 2 DS10s EV67 TRU64 V4, i have measured disk accesses using random and
sequential accesses ( a program repeats 1000 reads + write of 32 KB in raw mode ).

I used 2 different EV67 configurations:
- one with 2 IDEs 30 GB 7200 RPM (2 models: 53073W and MPD3108A)
- one with 2 SCSIs 18G / 10000 RPM, using 2 different models (RZ2ED and BD018635C4).


The first results shows the following throughputs ( input + output):

- sequential accesses:
   IDE disk drives (both models):   6 MB/s (10.6 ms mean time for read + write)
   SCSI 1:                          4.3 MB/s (14.8 ms ...)
   SCSI 2:                          6.5 MB/s (9.8 ms)


- random accesses:
  IDE:                         4.7 MB/s (13.6 ms)
  SCSI                         5 MB/s (12.8 ms)


Do you have an idea why the performance goes from
14.8 ms with one SCSI disk drive to 6.5 ms with the other model
( the RPM is the same, the seek time should not be significant with
sequential accesses) ?

I don't know how i can tune some SCSI drive parameters:
interleaving factor (1), usage of disk drive cache in write enabled ?
At what level can i tune the disk drive cache usage mode ?

I'll envoy if someone can give some comments.

Regards,
Eric

PS: i also made some measurements with the LSM V4 (BCL) on mirrored disks,
and with concurrencies on 2 IDE disks on the same channel. They
show for LSM half level of performance, and for IDE disk concurrency
a bad parallelism ( 20 % of gain only).

mailto: eric.chacron@alcatel.fr

.

