When i said should should I stick to nfs, I should have explained that 
all my previous experience had been with nfs and advfs was a step 
in the new direction. Although these are 2 different system, I still 
only see 2 ways to configure your disk(s) - discounting LSM - use nfs 
or advfs. I think I am confused on this part still because no-one else 
seems to consider the 2 systems comparable. I suppose you can 
have a mixture of both but I like to keep things uniform.


Thanx to: Richard Westlake, Joe Fletcher, Ettore Aldrovandi, Alan 
and Edward Branley.


I quote Alan because he dealt with all the questions in turn.


--------Alan---------------- 

I'll do the easy one first.


Q) Should I stick to nfs or are the real advantages to advfs.


 NFS is the Network File System.  AdvFS is one of two

 read-write local file systems supported by Tru64 UNIX.

 Both have their advantages and their place.  If you

 want centralized management of storage from some file

 server, then NFS is the way to go.  The disadvantages

 of NFS is that performance is limited by the speed of

 network links and you don't local control of the

 storage.  The advantage of a local file system is that

 it will generally be faster, but it has to be managed

 locally.


 I think your question is really; Local storage vs.

 remote storage?  Once you decide the answer to that

 you have to decide whether AdvFS or UFS is right for

 your local storage.


Q) Do I create 3 file domains on the boot disk?


 Arrange for the disk to have at least three partitions.  The

 usual installation allows having root, /usr and optionally

 /var on its own partition/domain.  If you want user space

 on the system disk, then you probably want to combine /usr

 and /var.  If you want three domains beyond the basic system

 disk, then just arrange to have sufficient extra partitions.


 Then, for each partition, create a domain:


  mkfdmn /dev/disk/dsk#x firstdomain

  mkfdmn /dev/disk/dsk#y seconddomain

  mkfdmn /dev/disk/dsk#z thirddomain


 Particular to the recommendation of having multiple domains

 on the same disk, it is unavoidable on the system disk; you

 have root and /usr if both are using AdvFS.  At least one

 problem of having multiple domains on a single disk is that

 you have multiple logs.  If the I/O is such that the log is

 frequently updated, this ensure that you're seeking all over

 the disk.  Odds are though, with three or more file systems

 on the disk, the I/O load will do this anyway.  Three logs

 may make it a little worse, but it is hard to keep I/O very

 localized short of not using large parts of a disk.


Q) If you do have a disk failure on a multiple volume domain, can you

recover any of the data from the good drive(s)?


 I suspect the salvage command will be able to sort through

 the remaining volumes to pick out most of what is left.  I

 wouldn't use this as a technique to avoid doing backups.


Q) Is there any way on knowing on which disk the data is stored?


 For a multi-volume domain, I believe one of the AdvFS

 commands will show what extents a file has and what

 volumes they're on.


Given the amount of external storage you have I would either

keep the H partition in reserve and not worry about the space

or reconfigure the system disk to use three or four partitions

for just the system disk stuff; root, swap, /usr and /var.


For the external disks you have some options:


 o  Multiple domains, probably one per disk.  You may

    want to set aside some partitions for small file

    systems and additional page/swap space.


 o  Use LSM to build the four disks into a single volume;

    a RAID-5, Mirrored stripe sets, one big stripe set.


 o  Make fewer domains, perhaps only one, and add the

    other disks to those as multi-volume domains.


AdvFS offers comparible performance to UFS and much quicker

recovery after a system crash.  It is also the basis for the

cluster file system in V5.  It isn't as tolerant of transient

I/O errors as UFS, but in the face of a major disaster, both

pretty much have to be restored from backup.



