<color><param>0100,0100,0100</param>It was a lead/connector problem as some of the responses guessed. 
After I exhausted my stock of leads and adapters I got a db9 female 
to db9 female lead from a friend and since then have been able to 
login in happily. Thanx to the following for the replies:

The ever present Dr Blinn

Dennis Breeden

Peyton Bland

David Hammet

Seldon Ball


I quote Seldon because I think he had a firm grasp of where the 
problem was and a good technical response although I don't have 
the cable making tool he suggested.

</color>Dp.

<FontFamily><param>Arial</param>You need to connect to COM1 using what is known as a "null 
modem" cable.

Others have reported intermittent connectivity, so I suspect that to 
be

reliable, it needs all of the 'ready' signals connected, not just

transmit, receive and ground.


We've had to make our own cables. Here's the pinout we use:


db9:                      db25


1: cd = carrier detect (ignore)

2: rd = receive data ---> 2: TD = transmit data

3: td = transmit data --> 3: RD = receive data

4: dtr = data term rdy -> 6: dsr = data set ready

5: gnd               ---> 1: gnd

6: dsr = data set rdy--> 20: dtr = data terminal ready

7: rts = Req to send -->  5: cts = clear to send

8: cts = clear to send -> 4: rts = Request to send

9: ri = ring indicator (ignore)


On most kinds of computers or terminals you can jumper the 

local RTS to CTS and DSR to DTR, so only a 3 wire cable

is needed, but some serial ports have timing issues when you do 
that.

Local jumpers do seem to work with DS10s, but I dunno about Wyse

terminals. (We use VT220s and DECserver 100s).


<FontFamily><param>Tahoma</param>Hi managers,

I have just received a new DS10 and am trying to get a serial 

connection to start configuring the system. I have tried numerous 

leads and connectors but can't get either a hyper-terminal 

connection or a connection using an WYSE terminal. I don't get an 

output to the monitor or an response from the keyboard. As we 

don't run X we didn't bother with a graphics card. The DS10 has 2 

coms ports, I believe its the com1/RMC port I should be using but I 

have tried both. 


Where do I begin?

Dp.

