I found the solution/workaround to this problem. I did not have a
default gateway in the /etc/routes file. After adding this the failover
worked. It should have worked without it though.

Regards

Udo de Boer




Admins,

I have the following questions. I have two machines in a 5.1 PK2
trucluster. I have an application that uses a network alias.

I was testing the network failover capabilities from the cluster by
unplugging the network cable from one of the machines. I see that the
machines is reacting on this because the arp entries for the cluster
alias go to the other machine. I also get a new gated.conf.member.X file

for the gated the daemon.

My application is not doing a failover because the cluster alias
subsystem should be able to handle this. BUT it doesn't. I can't get any

responce from the application if it is running on the node where cable
is pulled loose.

I think this problem is caused because the machine with no cable still
tries to route the packets for its subnet through the ethernet
interface. (netstat -rn) . I suspect to see a route through the memory
channel interface. But this is not in the new gated.conf.

Does anybody has this configuration working and tested? And does anybody

have seen the same problem.

regards

Udo de Boer



From tru64-unix-managers  Wed May 02 17:13:37 2001
From: john halewood <john () frumious ! unidec ! co ! uk>
Date: Wed, 02 May 2001 17:13:37 +0000
To: tru64-unix-managers
Subject: SUMMARY: HSZ70 detection problem
X-MARC-Message: https://marc.info/?l=tru64-unix-managers&m=102653647621630

(Problem was failure to detect a HSZ70 installed in an existing machine)

Thanks to the many people who responded, all much on the same lines. It 
turned out that the problem was simple and due to me not checking properly 
beforehand: when you boot genvmunix after adding hardware, make sure it 
really _is_ the original genvmunix and not in fact an old copy of vmunix that 
someone's left there. These sort of things can be a problem when you're 
working on unfamiliar machines.

cheers
john

p.s. as an aside, does anybody remember the location of the document that 
explains how the disk labels SRM sees (e.g. dkc100) map to /dev/rz17 etc? I 
know I've read it in the past but can't find it anywhere now. A web search 
didn't throw much light either

